Ops Phoenix — Autonomous SRE Agent
==================================

What is this?
-------------
A self-healing agent that runs every hour, queries production Loki for
error logs from reqsume-app-web-* and reqsume-api-* on app1/app2, asks
Claude to summarize them, and either opens a GitHub issue, comments on
an existing one, or stays silent based on thresholds (recurrence,
severity, rate limit, error count).

How it runs
-----------
- systemd timer `ops-phoenix-hourly.timer` fires at HH:07 every hour
- `Persistent=true` so missed fires run on next boot
- `RandomizedDelaySec=90` so multiple VMs don't fan out at the same instant
- Service is `Type=oneshot` under user `opsphoenix` (uid 999, nologin)
- `TimeoutStartSec=900` — anything hung past 15min gets SIGTERM'd
- One run at a time via `flock` on /var/lock/ops-phoenix.lock

Files
-----
- /opt/ops-phoenix/ — vendored skill code (git clone)
- /opt/ops-phoenix/bin/ops-phoenix-runner.sh — the wrapper
- /opt/ops-phoenix/.venv/ — python virtualenv
- /etc/ops-phoenix/config.json — observability + github + cicd
- /etc/ops-phoenix/ops-phoenix.env — non-secret defaults (systemd loads)
- /etc/ops-phoenix/secret — ANTHROPIC_API_KEY + BASE_URL
- /etc/ops-phoenix/gh-token — GitHub fine-grained PAT
- /etc/ops-phoenix/grafana-token — Grafana API token
- /var/lib/ops-phoenix/state/ — agent's $HOME (rate-limit + dedup state)
- /var/log/ops-phoenix/runs/ — one log per run, 30d retention
- /var/lib/ops-phoenix/heartbeat.prom — Prometheus textfile heartbeat

How to triage a failure
-----------------------
1. Check the timer: systemctl list-timers ops-phoenix-hourly.timer
2. Last 5 runs: ls -lt /var/log/ops-phoenix/runs/ | head -5
3. Tail the latest log: tail -100 /var/log/ops-phoenix/runs/<newest>.log
4. Service journal: journalctl -u ops-phoenix-hourly.service -n 50
5. Heartbeat: cat /var/lib/ops-phoenix/heartbeat.prom

How to disable (kill-switch)
----------------------------
Pick one. All take effect on the next timer fire.

 # Option A: env var (persistent across reboots)
 echo 'OPS_PHOENIX_DISABLED=1' | sudo tee -a /etc/ops-phoenix/ops-phoenix.env
 sudo systemctl daemon-reload

 # Option B: sentinel file (immediate, no daemon-reload needed)
 sudo touch /etc/ops-phoenix/disabled

 # Option C: nuclear
 sudo systemctl disable --now ops-phoenix-hourly.timer

To re-enable: undo whichever you did, then:
 sudo systemctl enable --now ops-phoenix-hourly.timer

How to manually trigger one run (without changing the schedule)
---------------------------------------------------------------
 sudo systemctl start ops-phoenix-hourly.service
 journalctl -u ops-phoenix-hourly.service -f

Who to page
